"bpsc-sec-q-0369","bpsc-tre-secondary","bpsc-sec-phy","bpsc-sec-phy-c2","bpsc-sec-phy-t8","","","A cylindrical metallic wire of length L and cross-sectional area A has electrical resistance R = 5 Ω. If the wire is stretched uniformly such that its length is doubled to 2L while its volume remains constant, what is its new resistance? (Problem 1)","20 Ω","10 Ω","2.5 Ω","More than one of the above","None of the above","A","When a wire of volume V = A × L is stretched uniformly to 2L, its cross-sectional area halves to A' = A / 2.
Original Resistance R = ρ × (L / A) = 5 Ω.
New Resistance R' = ρ × (L' / A') = ρ × (2L / (A/2)) = 4 × ρ × (L / A) = 4R = 4 × 5 = 20 Ω.","","original_practice","Verified Syllabus Practice","Moderate","2026-10-13T06:05:20Z"
