"bpsc-sec-q-0604","bpsc-tre-secondary","bpsc-sec-chem","bpsc-sec-chem-c1","bpsc-sec-chem-t3","","","How many molecules of water (H₂O, molar mass = 18 g/mol) are present in a sample containing 18 grams of pure water? (Problem 1)","1 × 6.022 × 10²³ molecules","0.5 × 6.022 × 10²³ molecules","18 × 10²³ molecules","More than one of the above","None of the above","A","Number of moles n = Mass (g) / Molar Mass (g/mol) = 18 / 18 = 1 mol.
Number of molecules = n × Avogadro's Number (N_A) = 1 × 6.022 × 10²³ molecules.","","original_practice","Verified Syllabus Practice","Easy","2026-10-13T06:05:20Z"
"bpsc-sec-q-0605","bpsc-tre-secondary","bpsc-sec-chem","bpsc-sec-chem-c1","bpsc-sec-chem-t3","","","How many molecules of water (H₂O, molar mass = 18 g/mol) are present in a sample containing 36 grams of pure water? (Problem 2)","2 × 6.022 × 10²³ molecules","1.0 × 6.022 × 10²³ molecules","36 × 10²³ molecules","More than one of the above","None of the above","A","Number of moles n = Mass (g) / Molar Mass (g/mol) = 36 / 18 = 2 mol.
Number of molecules = n × Avogadro's Number (N_A) = 2 × 6.022 × 10²³ molecules.","","original_practice","Verified Syllabus Practice","Easy","2026-10-13T06:05:20Z"
"bpsc-sec-q-0606","bpsc-tre-secondary","bpsc-sec-chem","bpsc-sec-chem-c1","bpsc-sec-chem-t3","","","How many molecules of water (H₂O, molar mass = 18 g/mol) are present in a sample containing 54 grams of pure water? (Problem 3)","3 × 6.022 × 10²³ molecules","1.5 × 6.022 × 10²³ molecules","54 × 10²³ molecules","More than one of the above","None of the above","A","Number of moles n = Mass (g) / Molar Mass (g/mol) = 54 / 18 = 3 mol.
Number of molecules = n × Avogadro's Number (N_A) = 3 × 6.022 × 10²³ molecules.","","original_practice","Verified Syllabus Practice","Easy","2026-10-13T06:05:20Z"
